BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2514@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261006T150717Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261006T100000
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261006T102000
SUMMARY:Probe events with typecast using BTF
LOCATION:Small Theatre\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:Tracing MC · Masami Hiramatsu · https://lpc.events/event/20/c
 ontributions/2514/\n\nBTF fetcharg has been introduced since v6.5 for fpro
 be and kprobe events for fetching function parameters by name\, and now we
  intrdouced typecast feature for BTF. This typecast feature is not only ca
 sting type\, but also\, the series supports nested typecasts\, container_o
 f()\, this_cpu_ptr()\, and "current" task structure access. With these fea
 tures\, we can access more context local data from dynamic trace events.\n
 I  would like to show how you can use these features and discuss what feat
 ures we can provide with BTF\, what limitations we have now etc.
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2514/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2511@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261006T150717Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261006T102200
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261006T104200
SUMMARY:Build ID + offset sampling in perf events
LOCATION:Small Theatre\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:Tracing MC · Ian Rogers · https://lpc.events/event/20/contrib
 utions/2511/\n\nPerformance event sampling traditionally captures virtual 
 addresses\, resolving them to file offsets and symbols post-facto via mmap
  metadata. Conversely\, BPF enables a direct approach: recording the file'
 s build ID and offset within the sample itself. While virtual addresses ar
 e smaller inline\, the required mmap event stream may lead to much larger 
 raw data files.\n\nIn this talk\, we examine this new sampling method and 
 its implementation trade-offs. The goal is to gather feedback to land the 
 pending patchset and explore hybrid sampling schemes that selectively comb
 ine both approaches to achieve the smallest possible footprint.
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2511/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2510@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261006T150717Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261006T104400
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261006T110400
SUMMARY:Developing Next Generation Perf Tools With Python
LOCATION:Small Theatre\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:Tracing MC · Alice Rogers\, Ian Rogers · https://lpc.events/e
 vent/20/contributions/2510/\n\nTraditionally\, the Linux perf tool has rel
 ied on a text user interface (TUI) based on libslang and embedded Python/P
 erl interpreters for scripting support. However\, developing robust user i
 nterfaces in C is tedious and error-prone\, and using perf itself as the i
 nterpreter integrates poorly with broader programming language ecosystems.
 \n\nIn this talk\, we will describe how we refactored perf into a set of l
 ibraries\, enabling it to be imported and used directly as a native Python
  module. Building on this foundation\, we will showcase new tools featurin
 g rich\, interactive console UIs\, culminating in a console-based flame gr
 aph analysis tool. This modular library approach dramatically accelerates 
 tool development\, paving the way for a more agile and extensible Linux pe
 rformance tooling ecosystem.
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2510/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2509@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261006T150717Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261006T110600
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261006T112600
SUMMARY:Bridging perf and ftrace: Recording perf in ftrace buffers and perf
  data to trace.dat conversion
LOCATION:Small Theatre\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:Tracing MC · Madhavan Srinivasan\, Steven Rostedt\, Tanushree 
 Shah · https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2509/\n\nThe tracefs sys
 tem allows for fast flexible tracing of events. Not only is it designed fo
 r speed (around 100 nanoseconds per event)\, it also has a fully functiona
 l filtering system and a way to build on events via probes and synthetic e
 vents.\n\nAllowing perf events to be injected into the tracefs ring buffer
  with these filters can allow for things like seeing how many cache misses
  happen between schedule switch events. Perf data could be recorded at any
  event and even filtered based on the value of the event fields.\n\nThere 
 is a [Proof of Concept][1] patch set that does this but the interface is v
 ery poor. This session will be about the best way to create the interface 
 to allow perf events to be injected into tracefs ring buffers.\n\nThe perf
  tool is best for statistical profiling and performance analysis\, and ftr
 ace/trace-cmd for event timeline visualization. While perf captures compre
 hensive performance data with minimal overhead\, understanding temporal re
 lationships and event sequences remains challenging through its native int
 erfaces. In contrast\, KernelShark provides powerful graphical insights bu
 t traditionally requires separate trace-cmd recordings. Since perf record 
 already captures tracepoint events alongside other performance metrics\, r
 unning multiple commands to obtain both statistical and timeline data is r
 edundant in production or embedded environments.\n\nThis discussion will b
 e about both moving perf events into trace.dat files as well as being able
  to inject perf events directly into the tracefs ring buffer.\n\n  [1]: ht
 tps://lore.kernel.org/linux-trace-kernel/20251118002950.680329246@kernel.o
 rg/\n  [2]: https://lore.kernel.org/linux-trace-kernel/20251118002950.6803
 29246@kernel.org/
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2509/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2480@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261006T150717Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261006T120000
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261006T122000
SUMMARY:Sharing trace infrastructure between in-tree and OOT tracers
LOCATION:Small Theatre\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:Tracing MC · Mathieu Desnoyers · https://lpc.events/event/20/
 contributions/2480/\n\nThe current situation regarding LTTng vs upstream L
 inux:\n\n1) There are maintainers who push for everything to be in tree\n2
 ) There are maintainers who are proponents for no-GPL-export when there ar
 e no in-tree users\n3) Most of the tracer common facilities are used by tr
 acers which do not compile as modules (only builtin)\n4) Linus Torvalds st
 ated that LTTng will stay out of tree\n\nAs a consequence\, LTTng has no w
 ay to use common tracer facilities\nwithout kernel patches.\n\nThis contex
 t is not favorable for collaboration of LTTng developers with upstream. It
  is hard to justify spending time on kernel infrastructure collaboration w
 hen the resulting APIs cannot be used by out-of-tree tracers.\n\nI am open
  to suggestions to improve this situation.
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2480/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2513@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261006T150717Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261006T122200
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261006T124200
SUMMARY:Lightweight Preempt and IRQ Tracepoints for Production RT Kernels
LOCATION:Small Theatre\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:Tracing MC · Wander Costa · https://lpc.events/event/20/contr
 ibutions/2513/\n\nDebugging latency issues in production RT kernels often 
 requires\nunderstanding where and why preemption and interrupts are being\
 ndisabled. Today\, enabling the `preempt_disable/enable` and\n`irq_disable
 /enable` tracepoints requires pulling in heavyweight\ninfrastructure — e
 ither the preemptoff/irqsoff latency tracers or the\nfull lockdep IRQ trac
 king — that carries too much overhead for\nproduction deployments. This 
 forces engineers to reproduce customer\nissues in lab environments\, which
  is often impractical for intermittent\nlatency problems.\n\nThis talk pre
 sents a patch series that introduces\n`CONFIG_TRACE_PREEMPT_TOGGLE` and `C
 ONFIG_TRACE_IRQFLAGS_TOGGLE`\, two\nnew user-selectable kernel options tha
 t enable these tracepoints\nindependently\, with minimal overhead. The tra
 cepoints are gated behind\nstatic keys.
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2513/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2512@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261006T150717Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261006T124400
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261006T130400
SUMMARY:Handling stack traces in tracing
LOCATION:Small Theatre\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:Tracing MC · Steven Rostedt · https://lpc.events/event/20/con
 tributions/2512/\n\nStack tracing of events can be very useful\, for both 
 kernel stack tracing as well as user space stack tracing. Stack traces can
  fill the buffer quickly with many duplicate stacks. Having a way to conso
 lidate them would make it possible to store even more data. There's been [
 efforts][1] to do this but how to implement it and the interface is still 
 an ongoing subject.\n\nOn top of that\, tracing user space can have the sa
 me issue. With the deferred stack trace\, it is now possible to do more wh
 en taking the stack trace (like reading the vma and finding what files are
  associated with the stack as well as reporting the actual file offset ins
 tead of the virtual memory of the task). Work for this has [been done][2] 
 as well but it also has [issues][3]. Figuring out how to implement this in
  a way that everyone is satisfied would be a goal of this topic.\n\n  [1]:
  https://lore.kernel.org/linux-trace-kernel/20260616064119.438063-1-lipeng
 fei28@xiaomi.com\n  [2]: https://lore.kernel.org/linux-trace-kernel/202508
 28180356.546256287@kernel.org/\n  [3]: https://lore.kernel.org/linux-trace
 -kernel/CAHk-=wi0EnrBacWYJoUesS0LXUprbLmSDY3ywDfGW94fuBDVJw@mail.gmail.com
 /
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2512/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2515@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261006T150717Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261006T130600
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261006T132600
SUMMARY:DEPT (DEPendency Tracker): A Path to Mainline and Solving the False
  Positive Challenge
LOCATION:Small Theatre\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:Tracing MC · Byungchul Park\, Yunseong Kim · https://lpc.even
 ts/event/20/contributions/2515/\n\nDEPT (DEPendency Tracker) is a runtime 
 dependency tracking framework\nthat detects potential deadlocks by trackin
 g wait/event relationships\nrather than lock acquisition order.  Unlike lo
 ckdep\, which is limited to\ntypical locking primitives\, DEPT can detect 
 deadlocks involving general\nsynchronization mechanisms such as folio lock
 s\, completions\, DMA fences\,\nand other wait/event-based patterns.\n\nTh
 is talk presents the current state of DEPT and addresses the critical\ncha
 llenge blocking its mainline inclusion: false positive handling\nthrough s
 ubsystem annotations.\n\nAny runtime dependency tracking tool faces the in
 herent challenge of\ndistinguishing real deadlocks from intentionally orde
 red synchronization\npatterns.  Lockdep also encountered this problem at i
 ts inception and\nsolved it through extensive subsystem-specific annotatio
 ns (lock classes\,\nsubclasses\, etc.).  DEPT currently lacks sufficient a
 nnotation for other\nthan typical locking primitives because it just start
 ed.\n\nThis talk proposes a collaborative path forward: (1) core framework
 \nstabilization to finalize annotation APIs\, (2) subsystem pilot programs
 \nwith maintainers if any (3) gradual mainline inclusion enabled\nincremen
 tally as annotations mature.\n\nI seek community discussion on: annotation
  design that minimizes\nmaintainer burden\, default behavior when annotati
 ons are missing\,\ntesting infrastructure\, and coexistence strategy with 
 lockdep.  Like\nlockdep before it\, achieving practical utility requires e
 xtensive\nsubsystem-specific annotations.  This talk charts the collaborat
 ive path\nto get there.
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2515/
END:VEVENT
END:VCALENDAR
